e that's very frustrating when  you feel like you know you've got the  offender but nobody even though they  were there they won't talk to you  unfortunately in some communities that  suffer from very high rates of homicide  there's also a corresponding lack of  trust in police both for the failure to  protect community members from homicide  and because of perhaps past police  misconduct against members of that  community and frankly over time there  can be cynicism both among the community  members and among the cops themselves  about the solvability of these crimes  and i think it's very important that we  try to push back on that cynicism as  much as possible  [Music]  there was this national movement of  distrust in police and reduced the  police and communities the interesting  thing that we've seen over this year as  our homicide  rates have gone where they've gone  is the community is actually not in that  same place  the community is actually asking where  are our police officers  we want the police officers in the  community  there isn't a single community that i've  gone into where they've said nah you  know what we don't want more police we  want more social workers to come in they  do want social workers to come in but  they also want to feel safe  we did see a major rise in homicide the  homicide rise in so many places was so  abrupt and it began  just about a day or two  after george floyd was killed  departments simply aren't able to adapt  as rapidly as the homicides are going up  and then if you add in the pandemic and  depletion in police ranks that makes  solution even more difficult our violent  crime reduction unit and our gun  recovery unit those two major units  that's been a challenge over the last  couple years in retention based on  everything that's going on in the  country and the general sentiment around  police as well as our ability to hire  across the country generally but  especially in communities that are  hardest hit by high homicide rates there  is this hopelessness that there's really  nothing that can be done and that it is  this chronic problem that is beyond sort  of solving  fortunately that's not the case  you know this year we're bad numbers  compared to years past year in the  district of columbia  but i also look at all the things that  we've done the things that we've been  able to accomplish and it could have  been a heck of a lot worse  there were three areas in the city where  there were 13 plus warrants that were  served within a very short period of  time and in all those areas we saw 60  percent reduction or more in violent  crime that lasted three to four months  and some still going on the mayor's made  her almost 60 million dollar investment  in our prevention side which is going to  have effect over the next year 18 months  to two years affecting those numbers too  i think  not satisfied not happy with the outcome  but i do think it could have been a heck  of a lot worse

